{
  "about": "PLACEHOLDER (docs/PLACEHOLDERS.md): (each surface also takes the engine's 2K normal and ORM maps for its texture now, so towns have relief and roughness.) This game's surfaces over the engine's art/textures/manifest.json (ArtPaths.json merges them). The engine has no `village`, `dungeon` or `cave` board themes (Curse of Strahd keeps its own village textures in its game folder), and a board theme with no floor is laid as the grid's checker, so every town showed a checkerboard. Until Crooked Moon's own tiles are painted (look P, seamless), each village surface borrows the engine's nearest texture: moss for grass, marsh mud for roads, stone flags for cobbles, plaster for house walls, dressed stone for slate and heath for thatch. A region's mood still swaps and tints the ground (art/atmosphere/moods.json ground_swap and ground_tint).",
"about": "PLACEHOLDER (docs/PLACEHOLDERS.md): This game's surfaces over the engine's art/textures/manifest.json (ArtPaths.json merges them). The engine has no `village`, `dungeon` or `cave` board themes (Curse of Strahd keeps its own village textures in its game folder), and a board theme with no floor is laid as the grid's checker, so every town showed a checkerboard. Until Crooked Moon's own tiles are painted (look P, seamless), each village surface borrows the engine's nearest texture: moss for grass, marsh mud for roads, stone flags for cobbles, plaster for house walls, dressed stone for slate and heath for thatch. A region's mood still swaps and tints the ground (art/atmosphere/moods.json ground_swap and ground_tint). The `real` surfaces are Poly Haven's scanned ground (CC0, art/sourced/polyhaven_forest) for the realistic wild places (world/look/real_nature.gd), with their own normal and AO-roughness-metal maps. `mountain/cliff` is swapped for the scanned mossy rock, so every cliff face in this game is realistic rock.",
